We employ a dissipative particle dynamics-type thermostat [57], with an effective temperature T, to account for energy dissipation
(8)
Fij(D)=γ⋅(1−drrt)2⋅(vij⋅rij) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} F_{i j}^{(D)}=\gamma \cdot\left(1-\frac{d r}{r t}\right)^{2} \cdot\left(\boldsymbol{v}_{i j} \cdot \boldsymbol{r}_{i j}\right) \tag{(8} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
and random fluctuations
(9)
Fij(R)=σξ⋅r^ij \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} F_{i j}^{(R)}=\sigma \xi \cdot \hat{\boldsymbol{r}}_{i j} \tag{(9} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where γ and σ are related to fulfill the fluctuation-dissipation theorem [58]. Also, background dissipation is taken into account as
(10)
FiB=−γbvi. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \boldsymbol{F}_{i}^{B}=-\gamma_{b} \boldsymbol{v}_{i} . \tag{10} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

g(r) The pair correlation function measures the likelihood to find two particles at a distance r. It is defined as
(12)
g(r)=⟨∑m,n≠mnS(t)ΘH(r−rmn(ti))ΘH(rmn(ti)−(r+δr))/Ω(r,t)⟩t, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} g(r)=\left\langle\sum_{m, n \neq m}^{n_{S}(t)} \Theta_{H}\left(r-r_{m n}\left(t_{i}\right)\right) \Theta_{H}\left(r_{m n}\left(t_{i}\right)-(r+\delta r)\right) / \Omega(r, t)\right\rangle_{t}, \tag{12} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where rmn(ti) = |rm(ti) − rn(ti)| is the pair distance, using the center of mass of cells, ΘH Heaviside functions, used to construct a measurement annulus of width δr, and Ω a normalization factor given by
Ω(r,t)=LXY2/[4(nS(t)−1)((r+δr)2−r2)] \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \Omega(r, t)=L_{X Y}^{2} /\left[4\left(n_{S}(t)-1\right)\left((r+\delta r)^{2}-r^{2}\right)\right]$$\end{document}
For randomly distributed particles g(r) = 1, which is why we display g(r) − 1 in Fig. 2 (A)+S3 (A). While we employ the pair correlation function for stem cell pairs, it can be generally be used to measure the pair correlation of other particle types, or cross type correlations.
